//############################## seam.tedesco.lib ##############################
// SEAM Davide Tedesco library. Its official prefix is `sdt`.
//
// The DSP of Davide Tedesco's works, re-read by SEAM as the specification for
// their hand-written ports (seam-ltm). Each entry says what the original used
// and why this version keeps it or departs from it, so that the library
// speaks both to us and to the composer.
//
// First work: *Studio sul Corpo d'Ombra #2* (Alice Cortegiani, Davide
// Tedesco, 2025), for contrabass clarinet, spherically diffused feedback
// devices and live electronics. The original is a Pure Data patch hosting
// Faust externals built with Faust 2.72.14 (`faust2puredata -double`).
//
// Requires faustlibraries 0965ea2 or later (filters.lib 1.11.1): compile with
// `-I <faustlibraries clone>`. The libraries bundled with Faust 2.88.0 still
// carry the direct-form `fi.lowpass`/`fi.highpass`.
//
// * [SSCDO#2 — LMO](#sscdo2-lmo)
// * [SSCDO#2 — delRM](#sscdo2-delrm)
//
// ## References
//
// * <https://github.com/s-e-a-m/faust-libraries/blob/master/src/seam.tedesco.lib>
// * <https://gitlab.com/DavideTedesco/studio_sul_corpo_dombra_numero_2>:
//   the original SSCDO#2 materials (Pd patch, Faust sources, score notes)
// * seam-ltm `logs/2026-09-29-sscdo2-ricognizione.md`: survey and decisions
// * seam-ltm `doc/study/sscdo2/lmo-bandfilter/`: the band-filter study, with renders
//##############################################################################
declare name "SEAM Davide Tedesco - Library";
declare version "0.1";
declare author "Giuseppe Silvi";
declare author "Davide Tedesco";
declare license "CC3";
import("seam.lib");
sdt = library("seam.tedesco.lib");

//================================ SSCDO#2 — LMO ===============================
// LMO (liveMultiOscs_30000_plain): a four-channel "oscillator" made of noise
// through a narrow band-pass, one band per driver of the tetrahedral STONED,
// and two such oscillators that beat: `lmoband`, `lmoosc`, `lmo`.
//
// What the original had and this version leaves out (2026-09-29):
// * the 1.015 frequency factor, a by-product of the old two-oscillator formula
//   (slider/2 + 1.03·slider/2): `f` is the band centre itself, and the cues
//   that were 96 and 96 → 111 Hz become 97.44 and 97.44 → 112.67 Hz, the
//   frequencies Davide actually heard;
// * the high-passed direct noise (volume 0 at start, on no fader: it never
//   sounded in the performance);
// * `fi.dcblockerat(20)` at the output, redundant after the 24th-order
//   highpass inside `lmoband`;
// * the +6 dB of the duplicated oscillator: `lmo` is normalised to one
//   oscillator, and the level is set by playing, not by compatibility.
//==============================================================================

//------------------------------`(sdt.)lmoband`---------------------------------
// The LMO band filter, the original's line unchanged:
// a 24th-order Butterworth highpass into a 24th-order Butterworth lowpass at
// the same frequency (the -0.0001 Hz offset is the original's; it moves
// nothing measurable).
//
// Two filters crossing at `f` make a band-pass that peaks at -6.02 dB, with a
// -3 dB band of 7.35 % of `f` (Q 13.6), -72 dB at half an octave, and a decay
// to -60 dB of about 0.31 s at 97 Hz: noise coloured into a pitch, with little
// ringing. `fi.bandpass` with the same edges is a different filter, whatever
// its order: it lays its skirts out in bandwidths, not in ratios of `f`, and
// at the same order rings 16 times longer.
//
// Since faustlibraries 0965ea2 (2026-09-26, J.O. Smith, issue #262) the
// sections of `fi.highpass`/`fi.lowpass` are state-variable filters instead of
// direct-form biquads: the same response, accurate in single precision at low
// `f`/SR, with no overshoot on frequency steps. The original ran the direct
// form in double, which was accurate in Pd; on the glissando of cue 2 the two
// forms differ by -74.7 dB.
//
// #### Usage
// ```
// _ : lmoband(f) : _
// ```
// Where:
//
// * `f`: band centre in Hz
//
// #### Test
// ```
// sdt = library("seam.tedesco.lib");
// lmoband_test = no.noise : sdt.lmoband(97.44);
// ```
//------------------------------------------------------------------------------
lmoband(f) = fi.highpass(24, f) : fi.lowpass(24, f - 0.0001);
// process = no.noise : lmoband(97.44);

//-------------------------------`(sdt.)lmoosc`---------------------------------
// One LMO oscillator on `N` channels: `N` noise streams, each through
// `lmoband`, channel `i` centred at `f + i` Hz as in the original.
//
// The noise is the standard `no.multinoise`, as in the original. Within one
// call its streams are decorrelated (max |r| 0.002 over 120 pairs), which is
// all a tetrahedral source needs. Its seed is fixed at 12345, so a second call
// returns the same noise: the original's second oscillator, at the same
// frequency, doubled the first (+6 dB) rather than adding to it (see `lmo`).
//
// The independence of the streams is audible. With one stream shared by the
// four channels, the `+ i` Hz offsets leave them almost identical (r = 0.998
// between adjacent channels at 1 kHz); with a stream per channel they are
// uncorrelated (|r| < 0.03) and the source opens up. Heard and measured on
// 2026-09-29 (seam-ltm `doc/study/sscdo2/lmo-streams/`).
//
// #### Usage
// ```
// lmoosc(N,f) : si.bus(N)
// ```
// Where:
//
// * `N`: number of channels (a constant numerical expression), 4 in SSCDO#2
// * `f`: band centre of channel 0 in Hz
//
// #### Test
// ```
// sdt = library("seam.tedesco.lib");
// lmoosc_test = sdt.lmoosc(4, 97.44);
// ```
//------------------------------------------------------------------------------
lmoosc(N, f) = no.multinoise(N) : par(i, N, lmoband(f + i));
// process = lmoosc(4, 97.44);

//--------------------------------`(sdt.)lmo`-----------------------------------
// The LMO: two oscillators that beat, on `N` channels.
//
// The original summed two `lmoosc` whose frequencies both came out at the same
// value, from the same noise: one oscillator at +6 dB. Davide's intention
// (2026-09-29): two bands that can beat; at 0 Hz apart they share the
// frequency and are decorrelated; at a distance `d` each moves `d/2` from the
// centre. So: `2N` streams from ONE `no.multinoise(2N)` call (two calls would
// give the same noise twice); streams `0..N-1` feed the bands at
// `f - d/2 + i`, streams `N..2N-1` those at `f + d/2 + i`; channel `i` sums
// its two bands. The offset `i` cancels in the difference, so every channel
// beats at `d`. Two independent noises add in power, hence `1/sqrt(2)`: the
// level equals one oscillator's at every `d` (within 0.2 dB, measured).
//
// A band of noise fluctuates by itself, its envelope carrying energy up to
// about 11 Hz at 97 Hz. The beat of two bands is a broad bump at `d` riding
// on that fluctuation: at 97 Hz it stands out by +3 dB at d = 10 Hz, +16 dB at
// 20 Hz, +35 dB at 40 Hz. Below about 10 Hz the second band widens the sound
// without beating. In SSCDO#2 `d` spans 0 to 50 Hz, for experiment
// (seam-ltm `doc/study/sscdo2/lmo-beats/`, with renders).
//
// #### Usage
// ```
// lmo(N,f,d) : si.bus(N)
// ```
// Where:
//
// * `N`: number of channels (a constant numerical expression), 4 in SSCDO#2
// * `f`: centre of channel 0 in Hz
// * `d`: distance between the two bands in Hz, 0 to 50 in SSCDO#2
//
// #### Test
// ```
// sdt = library("seam.tedesco.lib");
// lmo_test = sdt.lmo(4, 97.44, 20);
// ```
//------------------------------------------------------------------------------
lmo(N, f, d) = no.multinoise(2*N)
             : par(i, N, lmoband(f - d/2 + i)), par(i, N, lmoband(f + d/2 + i))
             :> par(i, N, /(sqrt(2)));
// process = lmo(4, 97.44, 20);

//=============================== SSCDO#2 — delRM ==============================
// delRM (delRM_duet): four channels, each processing only its own input from
// the TETRAREC. Channels 1 and 3 are a feed-forward comb, x + x[n-D]; channels
// 2 and 4 multiply x[n-D], x and the running sum of x, then compress.
//
// The delay D is DDELAY's (agreed with Davide, 2026-09-29, so that the same
// tool serves Studio sul Corpo d'Ombra #4 with two sources): a distance in
// metres, converted at 331.4 m/s and rounded to the next prime, tuned during
// the setup and left alone during the piece (`sma.imt2npsamp`). The original
// used seconds through `de.delay` with its arguments swapped (harmless: `min`
// picked the right one), and the float slider truncated 22 ms to 1055 samples
// at 48 kHz. The initial 22 ms is 7.291 m here: 1061 samples at 48 kHz.
//==============================================================================

//-----------------------------`(sdt.)delrmcomb`--------------------------------
// delRM's comb, channels 1 and 3: the input plus itself delayed by DDELAY's
// prime delay. The standard `fi.ff_comb` with both gains at 1 is exactly the
// original's `_ <: de.delay(...), _ :> _`.
//
// Its response is 2·|cos(pi·f·D/SR)|: peaks of +6.02 dB every SR/D Hz and
// notches halfway between. At 7.291 m (D = 1061 at 48 kHz) the peaks fall
// every 45.24 Hz and the notches at 22.62, 67.86, 113.10 Hz...: a dense comb
// across the register of the contrabass clarinet. On uncorrelated input the
// power rises by 3.01 dB.
//
// #### Usage
// ```
// _ : delrmcomb(mt) : _
// ```
// Where:
//
// * `mt`: distance in metres, 0 to 30 (DDELAY's range); 7.291 at the start
//
// #### Test
// ```
// sdt = library("seam.tedesco.lib");
// delrmcomb_test = no.noise : sdt.delrmcomb(7.291);
// ```
//------------------------------------------------------------------------------
delrmcomb(mt) = fi.ff_comb(1 << 15, sma.imt2npsamp(mt), 1, 1);
// process = no.noise : delrmcomb(7.291);

//------------------------------`(sdt.)delrmint`--------------------------------
// delRM's integrator: `sfi.leakyint(1)` scaled by 96000, the rate SSCDO#2 is
// played at.
//
// The original used `fi.integrator`, a sum of samples. On the DC of the real
// chain (3.22e-6 on the recording used for the study) its state grows
// linearly, past the note's own integral within a few minutes; a note arriving
// after five minutes finds the triple product 7 dB louder and 0.88 correlated
// with the plain 10·x[n-D]·x: channels 2 and 4 turned into another effect with
// the time the patch had been running. The leaky integrator forgets below 1 Hz,
// far below the clarinet's 29.7 Hz fundamental; scaled by 96000 it is the
// original's sum at 96 kHz above 1 Hz, and the same at any other rate.
//
// Why 96000 and not ma.SR: `*(ma.SR)` would cancel the normalisation and give
// back a sum of samples (a stable `fi.integrator`), identical at 96 kHz but
// 6 dB lower at 48 kHz before the compressor. The 11:1 compressor absorbs the
// level (-0.21 dB after it, measured) but not the depth of its work: the
// dynamics of channels 2 and 4 would change with the session's rate. Anchored
// at 96 kHz, delRM behaves the same in a 48 kHz rehearsal, in another piece,
// or in a student's session (the rule already applied to Di Scipio's 44.1 kHz).
//
// #### Usage
// ```
// _ : delrmint : _
// ```
//------------------------------------------------------------------------------
delrmint = sfi.leakyint(1) : *(96000);

//------------------------------`(sdt.)delrmrm`---------------------------------
// delRM's triple product, channels 2 and 4: the input delayed by DDELAY's
// prime delay, times the input, times its integral (`delrmint`). The ×10 and
// the compressor that follow belong to the next block.
//
// #### Usage
// ```
// _ : delrmrm(mt) : _
// ```
// Where:
//
// * `mt`: distance in metres, 0 to 30 (DDELAY's range); 7.291 at the start
//
// #### Test
// ```
// sdt = library("seam.tedesco.lib");
// delrmrm_test = os.osc(29.7) : sdt.delrmrm(7.291);
// ```
//------------------------------------------------------------------------------
delrmrm(mt) = _ <: sma.imnpdelay(1 << 15, mt), _, delrmint : *, _ : *;
// process = os.osc(29.7) : delrmrm(7.291);

//------------------------------`(sdt.)delrmdyn`--------------------------------
// delRM's dynamics after the triple product, channels 2 and 4: a gain of 10
// (+20 dB) into the standard mono compressor, ratio 11, threshold -24 dB,
// attack 30 ms, release 40 ms, as in the original. The compressor's detector
// and time constants are in seconds, so it behaves the same at every rate.
//
// The triple product is cubic in the input: 6 dB more from the clarinet is
// 18 dB more into the compressor, which already receives it about 45 dB above
// threshold. At 11:1 it works as a limiter near -20 dBFS (measured in seam-ltm
// doc/study/sscdo2/delrm-rm/): the loudness of channels 2 and 4 hardly follows
// the playing, and what the playing changes is the texture.
//
// #### Usage
// ```
// _ : delrmdyn : _
// ```
//------------------------------------------------------------------------------
delrmdyn = *(10) : co.compressor_mono(11, -24, 0.03, 0.04);
